rules/schrybwys.md and rules/schrybwys-compact.md
-------------------------------------------------
Licensed CC BY-SA 4.0; see rules/LICENSE. These describe the codified "schriftsprach-nah" spelling system for Bernese
German. That system is not ours. It was established by:

  Werner Marti, "Bärndütschi Schrybwys" (Francke, Bern, 1978) and
  "Berndeutsch-Grammatik" (Francke, Bern, 1985)

  Otto von Greyerz / Ruth Bietenhard, "Berndeutsches Wörterbuch"
  (10th edition, Cosmos, 2017)

and is summarised for the public by berndeutsch.ch, notably in:

  Ursula Pinheiro-Weber, "Bärndütsch schrybe: schriftsprach-nach" (2021),
  licensed CC BY-ND 4.0
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-schriftsprach-nah-v1.pdf

  Hans Jürg Zingg, "Bärndütsch schrybe: usschpraach-naach!" (2021), the
  aussprach-nah counterpart, same licence. Its title is quoted here as the
  document itself spells it. This file previously rendered it in the
  schriftsprach-nah system, which is the one thing that document is not.
  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/doc/berndeutsch-schreiben-aussprach-nah-v1.pdf

  https://www.berndeutsch.ch/pages/schrybe (the free summary page)

The rules a spelling system prescribes are facts about that system, and they
are stated here in our own words and our own arrangement.

About the examples, measured by a script rather than asserted by a human. This
paragraph was written by hand three times and was wrong all three times: it
named words as shared that are not in the PDF at all, it quoted a count that
had drifted after an edit, and it flatly claimed no phrase appeared in both
while four did. So the claim is no longer hand-written.

`scripts/pdf-overlap` fetches the PDF, extracts the illustrative vocabulary and
reports the overlap together with every shared word sequence. It takes --file,
so the compact rulebook and the checklist embedded in the hook, which are
copies of the same content and drift apart, are measured the same way. For the
hook it measures the CHECKLIST string rather than the .py around it, and says
so in the line quoted below: pointed at the source file it counted 943 words of
