For the two copies the script says so itself on stderr: below twenty backticked
words it measures EVERY word of the text instead, so "illustrative words: 212"
for the compact rulebook counts its prose as well as its examples. The headline
percentage for those two is therefore a whole-text overlap, not an example
overlap, and it is quoted here as the script reports it rather than relabelled.

Between a quarter and a half of the illustrative vocabulary is shared, and
pretending otherwise would be silly. These are the standard illustrations for
these rules, for several rules they are close to the only ones that exist, and
we learned the rules from that document.

The sequence classification above is the script's, not ours. It labels a
sequence a citation when every word in it only ever names a source (an author,
a document, a publisher, a licence), and LETTERS when the sequence is nothing
but single letters, which happens because both texts NAME letters when they
discuss spelling and the word splitter drops the punctuation between them:
"b d" comes from "a soft `b`, `d` or `g`", "n i" from "Wo-n-i", "u l" from
"u + l". The script then counts what is left over and prints that count.

Across all three files, two sequences are left over. "wi ne" is the linking-n
construction the rule teaches rather than an example anyone chose. "nd wird" is
the German statement of the rule itself: both texts write that `nd` wird `ng`,
and there is no other way to say it in German. The classifier called that one a
citation for a round, because `wird` had been listed as a citation word; it is
an ordinary German auxiliary and does not belong in that list.

This paragraph used to state that partition by hand, and got it wrong. It said
sixteen of the seventeen were citations and named "wi ne" as the only
exception, while the three letter pairs sat contradicting it thirty lines
higher up in this same file. That was the fourth time a hand-written sentence
here disagreed with the measurement printed above it, so the sentence was
replaced by a column in the output. There is no longer a claim here for a
future edit to falsify.

Getting there took work, and every step of it was found by measurement rather
than by re-reading. Five example phrases once stood verbatim in both ("wi ne
Bär", the three spellings of "Wi ni gseit ha", "mir luege aa") and are
replaced. Several example lists carried the PDF's ordering, which surfaced as
shared sequences ("lila d klinik", "stei spile starch", "umespringe
verstecke") and are reordered. One word, "schryne", was our typo for "schyne".

What is ours is the prose and the arrangement. The rulebook opens with a
section that document does not have ("What gives a text away"), and the
consonant section is ordered by what each phenomenon is rather than by the
PDF's numbered sequence. Neither document is redistributed with this
repository.

